@@ -14,7 +14,7 @@ Copy `.env.production.example` to `.env.production` on the server. Set the domai
 
 Caddy limits backend request bodies to 16 MiB and gives the client 30 seconds to send the body. Django checks the declared size before form parsing. Multipart forms can use the full 16 MiB. URL-encoded forms are limited to 64 KiB, and the generator and field checks accept at most 8 KiB of JSON. Django uses only the in-memory upload handler. Caddy streams requests to a single synchronous Gunicorn worker and can return 503 while it is busy. The worker timeout is a separate backstop from Caddy's HTTP timeouts.
 
-Base64 keeps the 10 MiB data limit and returns one attachment response. The browser retains it as a Blob on the page, shows at most the first 8 KiB of text, and attempts image preview only for recognised raster files up to 1 MiB. Copying and downloading use the full result. The application does not write request contents or results to persistent storage, and its responses use `Cache-Control: no-store`. These bounds describe application buffering and do not measure peak container memory.
+Base64 accepts up to 10 MiB before encoding or after decoding. The larger Base64 text file may be uploaded for decoding as long as the entire multipart request fits within 16 MiB. Base64 returns one attachment response. The browser retains it as a Blob on the page, shows at most the first 8 KiB of text, and attempts image preview only for recognised raster files up to 1 MiB. JSON also has a bounded preview, with full copy and download in the browser. The application does not write request contents or results to persistent storage, and its responses use `Cache-Control: no-store`. These bounds describe application buffering and do not measure peak container memory.
 
